WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:05.380
Netflix's adaptation of the travels and travails
of the mutated monster-hunter Geralt are based

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:09.850
on Andrzej Sapkowski's long-running series
of novels and short stories, and not on the

00:00:09.850 --> 00:00:11.680
popular Witcher video game trilogy.

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:16.000
But the CD Projekt Red games are a massive
hit across the world, and the Witcher series'

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:18.720
writer and showrunner Lauren Schmidt Hissrich
knows it.

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:22.920
That's precisely why, as Hissrich began adapting
the high-fantasy world and laying out her

00:00:22.929 --> 00:00:27.050
season 1 narrative, she was sure to leave
some room for an obvious nod or two to those

00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:29.500
who entered the fabled world through their
consoles.

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:34.140
While Hissrich pulled several iconic monsters,
men, and moments from Sapkowski's expansive

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:38.710
novels, she made sure to slip in a bathing
scene between lovers Geralt of Rivia and mage

00:00:38.710 --> 00:00:43.180
Yennefer that had become wildly popular among
fans of The Witcher 3: Wild Hunt.

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:47.420
"Fishing for a Djinn seems an extreme measure
to remedy sleeplessness."

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:51.620
"When extreme measures seem reasonable, yes.

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:53.140
I'm desperate."

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:58.000
The "bathtub Geralt" scene is featured during
the game's opening sequence and captures an

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:01.980
intimate, bare-all moment between the monster
slayer and his on-again-off-again partner

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:02.500
Yennefer.

00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:06.500
By the looks of it, they've both somewhat
settled down with Geralt's charge, Princess

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:07.000
Ciri

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:12.490
At San Diego Comic-Con 2019, Hissrich first
alluded that some form of the scene would

00:01:12.490 --> 00:01:13.720
appear on the Netflix show.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:15.000
She told iO9:

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:17.240
"There is a bathtub this season.

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:18.460
There is a bathtub.

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:21.880
I won't tell you who's in the bathtub, but
there is a bathtub."

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:26.100
For those who were new to The Witcher universe
when the Netflix series first dropped, the

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:29.990
moment might have felt like one among many
gratuitous shirtless scenes, but it actually

00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:31.500
holds a broader significance.

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:36.820
The "bathtub Geralt" scene opens The Witcher
3: Wild Hunt, and is somewhat beloved for,

00:01:36.820 --> 00:01:41.130
of course, giving gamers a glimpse of Geralt
both in the nude and providing a rare, intimate

00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:42.900
moment with the woman he loves.

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:47.190
Relaxing in a hot bath within his chamber
at the Witcher Academy known as Kaer Morhen

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:51.940
in the game Geralt is seen with his feet and
arms hanging over the sides of a wooden tub.

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:57.040
We then see him jolt up and remove a crustacean-like
creature from his, erm… private parts.

00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:04.820
"You know I don't find that amusing."

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:11.000
It takes only a moment for it to become clear
the animal isn't real and is instead a magical

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:15.220
prodding delivered by the naked but strategically
positioned sorceress Yennefer.

00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:19.120
Geralt promised Ciri he'd train with her,
and Yennefer wants him to get on with it,

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:23.200
so the moment ends with Geralt begrudgingly
exiting the tub, walking over to the mage,

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:26.820
and engaging in a passionate kiss before stomping
off to meet up with Ciri.

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:31.300
It's not particularly long and not especially
exciting, but it does offer up a different

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:36.280
side to the life of Geralt one where he doesn't
have to be all sword-wielding and blood splatter.

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:40.700
The scene's a slice-of-life moment that helps
flesh out The Witcher games' key characters.

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:45.000
The moment within the Netflix series is somewhat
different, with Geralt and Yennefer mostly

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:50.240
teasing one another as the issue around the
Djinn an air genie that causes chaos looms.

00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:54.500
The sorceress even joins the monster slayer
of Rivia in the steaming hot soak.

00:02:54.510 --> 00:02:58.760
But like The Witcher 3 sequence, it's mostly
about the verbal tit-for-tat Geralt and Yennefer

00:02:58.760 --> 00:02:59.560
are known for.

00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:03.840
The inclusion of the scene in the show was,
for showrunner Hissrich, a very intentional

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:05.720
olive branch for video game fans.

00:03:06.020 --> 00:03:07.020
She told GamesRadar:

00:03:07.460 --> 00:03:11.460
"It is fun to visually throw a wink and a
nod at video game fans, to say, 'We see you

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:11.960
too.

00:03:12.140 --> 00:03:12.900
We know you're here.'

00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:15.460
And we want them to be happy with what we're
doing as well."

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:19.880
While it's not clear if we'll get any more
steamy scenes featuring Geralt the bathing

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:24.280
beauty on the show's second season, season
one's bare-chested, peeking knee-cap action

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:28.340
will surely suffice in the meantime for gamers
and casual oglers alike.

